            <ArticleTitle>Rare case of multiple aggressive vertebral hemangiomas.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>439-42</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.4103/0976-9668.160030</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A case of a 42-year-old man with back pain associated with left radiculopathy and infiltrative mass involving the T12 and L5 vertebrae is described. Magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) and computed tomography indicated aggressive hemangiomas involving the T12 and L5 vertebrae. Three-year follow-up by imaging indicated minimally increased aggressiveness of the L5 lesion without any significant change in appearance of the T12 lesion thus, confirming the initial diagnosis of multiple aggressive vertebral hemangiomas.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
